1
00:00:05,657 --> 00:00:07,703
[papers rustling]

2
00:00:07,790 --> 00:00:11,968
Oh, your, uh...identification.

3
00:00:12,055 --> 00:00:16,799
Congratulations. You are once
again a consultant for the CBI.

4
00:00:16,886 --> 00:00:19,497
Thank you.
What about Lisbon?

5
00:00:19,584 --> 00:00:21,978
Agent Lisbon
remains on suspension

6
00:00:22,065 --> 00:00:23,414
pending departmental
investigation

7
00:00:23,501 --> 00:00:26,983
of her actions
in the Red John matter.

8
00:00:27,070 --> 00:00:28,941
'You'll be working with..'

9
00:00:29,029 --> 00:00:30,769
Ray Haffner from now on.

10
00:00:30,856 --> 00:00:33,946
He's an excellent agent.
Top-flight team.

11
00:00:34,034 --> 00:00:36,427
- You're dumping her.
- Not at all.

12
00:00:36,514 --> 00:00:41,780
Uh, we're conducting a careful
and scrupulously fair review.

13
00:00:41,867 --> 00:00:43,869
You're rehiring me
and you're firing Lisbon.

14
00:00:43,956 --> 00:00:46,220
That's a little...perverse,
don't you think?

15
00:00:46,307 --> 00:00:48,265
No, see, you close cases.

16
00:00:48,352 --> 00:00:50,311
Agent Lisbon ran a covert
operation that led

17
00:00:50,398 --> 00:00:53,531
to the deaths of two police
officers and one FBI agent.

18
00:00:53,618 --> 00:00:56,056
There just has to be
some accountability there.

19
00:00:56,143 --> 00:00:58,145
My operation.

20
00:00:58,232 --> 00:00:59,885
That was my operation.

21
00:00:59,972 --> 00:01:01,844
'Well, that's
exactly the problem.'

22
00:01:01,931 --> 00:01:04,368
'We expect better supervision
from our senior agents'

23
00:01:04,455 --> 00:01:06,544
which is why Lisbon
is being investigated

24
00:01:06,631 --> 00:01:09,460
and her team reassigned
to other posts.

25
00:01:09,547 --> 00:01:13,464
Uh, Haffner and his team
already caught another case.

26
00:01:13,551 --> 00:01:16,206
Here's the address.
You can hit the ground running.

27
00:01:16,293 --> 00:01:18,600
No, I don't think so.

28
00:01:18,687 --> 00:01:21,472
No? What-what do you mean?

29
00:01:21,559 --> 00:01:22,865
Just what I said.

30
00:01:25,041 --> 00:01:26,695
I'll get right on this.

31
00:01:31,656 --> 00:01:34,355
[indistinct chattering on radio]

32
00:01:34,442 --> 00:01:36,661
- Jane, right?
- Yup.

33
00:01:36,748 --> 00:01:38,315
- Ray Haffner.
- Hi.

34
00:01:38,402 --> 00:01:40,839
I'm-I'm hoping we can
jump in and do the job.

35
00:01:40,926 --> 00:01:42,102
- Sound good?
- Sure.

36
00:01:42,189 --> 00:01:43,712
Great.

37
00:01:43,799 --> 00:01:45,975
'This is Patrick Jane.
Jane, this is Rick Tork.'

38
00:01:46,062 --> 00:01:47,368
- How are you?
- Hi.

39
00:01:47,455 --> 00:01:48,934
- Anthony Niskin.
- Nice to meet you.

40
00:01:49,021 --> 00:01:50,197
- Hi.
- And Ed Masterson.

41
00:01:50,284 --> 00:01:52,721
- Hello. Hi.
- Yeah.

42
00:01:52,808 --> 00:01:54,810
[indistinct chattering on radio]

43
00:01:54,897 --> 00:01:57,378
- I'll just..
- Absolutely.

44
00:01:57,465 --> 00:02:00,816
[Masterson]
'The victim's name
is Markus Kuzmenko.'

45
00:02:00,903 --> 00:02:03,993
'Got hit with something heavy
on the left side of his head.'

46
00:02:04,080 --> 00:02:06,300
'No sign of the murder weapon.'

47
00:02:06,387 --> 00:02:08,693
May I? Thank you.

48
00:02:08,780 --> 00:02:11,174
[Masterson]
'Gardener found the body
when he came today.'

49
00:02:11,261 --> 00:02:13,655
Coroner says it happened
around 11 last night.

50
00:02:13,742 --> 00:02:15,439
Haven't found any witnesses.

51
00:02:19,530 --> 00:02:21,053
Jane?

52
00:02:21,141 --> 00:02:22,185
Hmm?

53
00:02:22,272 --> 00:02:23,578
Any thoughts?

54
00:02:23,665 --> 00:02:24,840
Yeah.

55
00:02:28,148 --> 00:02:30,628
"The years teach us patience.

56
00:02:30,715 --> 00:02:32,152
"The shorter our time,
the greater

57
00:02:32,239 --> 00:02:34,980
our capacity for waiting."

58
00:02:35,067 --> 00:02:37,069
That's pretty deep.
Is it Buddha?

59
00:02:37,157 --> 00:02:38,680
Elizabeth Taylor.

60
00:02:38,767 --> 00:02:40,986
This man's dead.
I'm all but certain of it.

61
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:47,297
What?

62
00:02:47,384 --> 00:02:48,690
[sighs]
Alright then.

63
00:02:49,517 --> 00:02:52,389
[theme music]

64
00:03:00,310 --> 00:03:01,920
Thank you.

65
00:03:06,708 --> 00:03:08,013
Mmm.

66
00:03:08,100 --> 00:03:09,493
How's the arm?

67
00:03:09,580 --> 00:03:11,669
Better. Yeah, physical
therapy's helping.

68
00:03:11,756 --> 00:03:12,714
Good.

69
00:03:12,801 --> 00:03:14,890
[sighs]

70
00:03:14,977 --> 00:03:16,805
Tell me, uh

71
00:03:16,892 --> 00:03:19,982
what do you know about
this guy Ray Haffner?

72
00:03:20,069 --> 00:03:21,766
You're back at work?

73
00:03:21,853 --> 00:03:24,465
Yeah, you know,
just consulting.

74
00:03:26,118 --> 00:03:28,338
Haffner, uh..

75
00:03:28,425 --> 00:03:30,775
...word is he's tough,
by the book.

76
00:03:30,862 --> 00:03:32,255
I think he started out

77
00:03:32,342 --> 00:03:34,388
in the FBI organized
crime division.

78
00:03:34,475 --> 00:03:36,216
He had a couple
of high-profile busts

79
00:03:36,303 --> 00:03:39,828
using wiretaps and creative
surveillance techniques.

80
00:03:39,915 --> 00:03:41,177
Surveillance, huh?

81
00:03:41,264 --> 00:03:43,179
- Yeah.
- Hmm.

82
00:03:43,266 --> 00:03:44,485
Why do you wanna know?

83
00:03:44,572 --> 00:03:47,009
Eh, just curious.

84
00:03:47,096 --> 00:03:49,881
- Oh.
- Can I take this?

85
00:03:49,968 --> 00:03:51,753
- You're leaving?
- Yeah, new case.

86
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:54,756
I, uh, I gotta man the phones,
hit the sidewalk.

87
00:03:54,843 --> 00:03:56,801
- You know how it is.
- Yeah, I know.

88
00:03:56,888 --> 00:03:58,107
- Thanks for coming by.
- Pleasure.

89
00:03:58,194 --> 00:04:00,065
Yeah.

90
00:04:00,152 --> 00:04:02,677
Just so you know,
Jane, I'm okay.

91
00:04:02,764 --> 00:04:03,808
About?

92
00:04:03,895 --> 00:04:05,332
Losing my job.

93
00:04:05,419 --> 00:04:07,159
I'm not gonna say
I like it

94
00:04:07,247 --> 00:04:10,293
but we got Red John.
It was worth it.

95
00:04:10,380 --> 00:04:12,774
Don't be silly.
A, you haven't lost your job.

96
00:04:12,861 --> 00:04:14,515
The hearing's a formality.

97
00:04:14,602 --> 00:04:16,734
And B, we didn't
get Red John.

98
00:04:16,821 --> 00:04:19,824
Timothy Carter told
you he was Red John.

99
00:04:19,911 --> 00:04:20,912
He lied.

100
00:04:20,999 --> 00:04:21,957
'Carter must've known him'

101
00:04:22,044 --> 00:04:23,524
because he told me things that

102
00:04:23,611 --> 00:04:25,526
only my wife and daughter's
killer could know.

103
00:04:25,613 --> 00:04:26,918
But he's not the man.

104
00:04:27,005 --> 00:04:29,356
- That man's still out there.
- Jane.

105
00:04:29,443 --> 00:04:31,967
Someone told that security
guard to take Carter's gun

106
00:04:32,054 --> 00:04:34,491
and phone, then killed him.

107
00:04:34,578 --> 00:04:36,754
Who would do that
besides Red John?

108
00:04:36,841 --> 00:04:39,017
Ergo, Timothy Carter
is not Red John.

109
00:04:39,104 --> 00:04:41,324
Carter had to have known
there was a possibility

110
00:04:41,411 --> 00:04:43,370
you'd kill him.
You always said you would.

111
00:04:43,457 --> 00:04:45,894
So maybe he asked
a guard to take the gun

112
00:04:45,981 --> 00:04:47,722
and the cell phone
if you did.

113
00:04:47,809 --> 00:04:49,245
- Why?
- To mess with your head.

114
00:04:49,332 --> 00:04:50,594
And it's working.

115
00:04:50,681 --> 00:04:53,031
You're looking for a man
you already killed.

116
00:04:53,118 --> 00:04:54,598
Carter's wife is in custody.

117
00:04:54,685 --> 00:04:56,861
- 'She said anything?'
- Nothing.

118
00:04:56,948 --> 00:04:59,168
When she does talk,
you know what she'll say?

119
00:04:59,255 --> 00:05:01,562
That you shot
and killed Red John.

120
00:05:01,649 --> 00:05:03,433
Good for you.

121
00:05:03,520 --> 00:05:05,087
No.

122
00:05:05,174 --> 00:05:06,131
Thanks for the tea.

123
00:05:06,218 --> 00:05:07,219
What do you mean?

124
00:05:07,307 --> 00:05:09,352
Uh, lovely to see you.

125
00:05:09,439 --> 00:05:10,875
Keep working that bum wing.

126
00:05:15,184 --> 00:05:17,839
Keys, change, anything metal.

127
00:05:17,926 --> 00:05:19,841
Thank you.

128
00:05:19,928 --> 00:05:22,104
[Rigsby]
Thanks.

129
00:05:22,191 --> 00:05:23,584
- Jane.
- New assignment?

130
00:05:23,671 --> 00:05:26,108
Just temporary.
Security's shorthanded.

131
00:05:26,195 --> 00:05:27,892
At least that's
what they tell me.

132
00:05:27,979 --> 00:05:30,721
Cho got a call today. Not sure
where they're putting him.

133
00:05:30,808 --> 00:05:33,942
Grace is still waiting to hear.
At least we still have jobs.

134
00:05:36,988 --> 00:05:38,860
You, uh, talk to Lisbon?

135
00:05:38,947 --> 00:05:39,948
Uh, yes.

136
00:05:40,035 --> 00:05:41,906
We're gonna have a..

137
00:05:41,993 --> 00:05:43,473
...a little secret
meeting later.

138
00:05:43,560 --> 00:05:45,432
- Tell Cho and Van Pelt.
- Secret meeting?

139
00:05:45,519 --> 00:05:47,825
- About what?
- I'll tell you then.

140
00:05:50,524 --> 00:05:51,481
Keys.

141
00:05:51,568 --> 00:05:54,441
[instrumental music]

142
00:06:05,365 --> 00:06:08,933
Hey, Tork, you're still
20 short on Tuesday's dinner.

143
00:06:09,020 --> 00:06:10,892
Man up, my brother.

144
00:06:10,979 --> 00:06:12,589
Yeah.

145
00:06:16,027 --> 00:06:18,247
Jane, heard you used
to be in the circus.

146
00:06:18,334 --> 00:06:21,206
- Is that right?
- Well, um, not exactly.

147
00:06:27,082 --> 00:06:29,258
I like your shoes.

148
00:06:29,345 --> 00:06:31,434
Yeah.

149
00:06:31,521 --> 00:06:33,480
Hey.

150
00:06:33,567 --> 00:06:36,134
I doubt Niskin meant
anything, by the way.

151
00:06:36,221 --> 00:06:37,484
What do you mean?

152
00:06:37,571 --> 00:06:40,487
"You're short." "Man up."

153
00:06:40,574 --> 00:06:43,272
I don't think
he was referring to..

154
00:06:43,359 --> 00:06:44,316
No.

155
00:06:44,404 --> 00:06:45,709
I mean..

156
00:06:49,147 --> 00:06:50,322
...no.

157
00:06:52,237 --> 00:06:54,936
What have we got on the victim?

158
00:06:55,023 --> 00:06:58,592
Kuzmenko was a naturalized
citizen originally from Estonia.

159
00:06:58,679 --> 00:07:00,985
Was a swimmer on
the Estonian National Swim Team

160
00:07:01,072 --> 00:07:04,293
came over to the US 2002,
no family here.

161
00:07:04,380 --> 00:07:07,122
Stop playing with that little
ball. You're driving me crazy.

162
00:07:07,209 --> 00:07:10,647
[Masterson]
He worked as a personal trainer
at a gym called Work-It.

163
00:07:10,734 --> 00:07:12,475
'Got his picture
off their web site.'

164
00:07:12,562 --> 00:07:16,131
I know that gym.
A lot of bigwigs work out there.

165
00:07:16,218 --> 00:07:19,482
Hey. Jane.

166
00:07:19,569 --> 00:07:22,137
Your thing's reading people.
What's this guy say to you?

167
00:07:22,224 --> 00:07:24,487
I'd guess he was a narcissist

168
00:07:24,574 --> 00:07:26,446
and a serial seducer.

169
00:07:26,533 --> 00:07:28,796
How do you get that?

170
00:07:28,883 --> 00:07:30,362
A jock with manicured nails

171
00:07:30,450 --> 00:07:32,582
too much cologne,
too tight of pants

172
00:07:32,669 --> 00:07:34,845
and he drives
a 20-year-old muscle car.

173
00:07:36,673 --> 00:07:38,806
Okay. We'll see
if that's useful.

174
00:07:38,893 --> 00:07:41,461
Masterson, you and Tork, you
cover Kuzmenko's neighborhood.

175
00:07:41,548 --> 00:07:44,159
Niskin, you run his
phone and e-mails.

176
00:07:44,246 --> 00:07:48,250
- I'll cover the gym.
- I'll go with you.

177
00:07:48,337 --> 00:07:49,556
Sure. You can come.

178
00:07:49,643 --> 00:07:51,993
Agent Haffner,
you asked to see me?

179
00:07:52,080 --> 00:07:55,126
Yeah, Cho, let's talk in my
office. Meet you at the gym.

180
00:07:58,608 --> 00:07:59,914
Sit down.

181
00:08:01,785 --> 00:08:04,440
I saw your old team member
downstairs...Rigsby?

182
00:08:06,268 --> 00:08:09,053
- 'That's tough.'
- I guess.

183
00:08:09,140 --> 00:08:12,622
I know your reputation, Cho.
I read your file.

184
00:08:12,709 --> 00:08:14,450
'You're a good cop'

185
00:08:14,537 --> 00:08:17,584
but this Red John thing
really pissed Bertram off.

186
00:08:17,671 --> 00:08:19,499
Puts you in a bad spot.

187
00:08:23,067 --> 00:08:25,287
- What's your take on Jane?
- What do you mean?

188
00:08:25,374 --> 00:08:28,116
- You trust him?
- 'Depends.'

189
00:08:28,203 --> 00:08:29,770
Think he's smarter than you?

190
00:08:29,857 --> 00:08:31,728
Yes.

191
00:08:31,815 --> 00:08:34,862
- Think he's smarter than me?
- Yes.

192
00:08:34,949 --> 00:08:36,341
You're probably right.

193
00:08:36,428 --> 00:08:38,387
I read Jane's file.
I know what'll happen.

194
00:08:38,474 --> 00:08:40,258
He's gonna go behind my back..

195
00:08:40,345 --> 00:08:42,565
...lie to me,
run a scam on me, right?

196
00:08:42,652 --> 00:08:45,046
- Pretty much.
- 'He's also gonna close cases.'

197
00:08:45,133 --> 00:08:47,309
I get that too.
I can work with Jane.

198
00:08:47,396 --> 00:08:49,659
I just need some help

199
00:08:49,746 --> 00:08:52,793
'someone who knows him, someone
who can tell me what's going on'

200
00:08:52,880 --> 00:08:55,099
warn me when something's coming.

201
00:08:55,186 --> 00:08:56,927
'I'm not talking about
betraying a confidence.'

202
00:08:57,014 --> 00:08:59,495
- I wanna be kept in the loop.
- What's the difference?

203
00:08:59,582 --> 00:09:01,802
You help me, you're
at your desk working cases.

204
00:09:01,889 --> 00:09:03,543
You don't, you're
downstairs working the lobby

205
00:09:03,630 --> 00:09:06,154
with your pal Rigsby.

206
00:09:06,241 --> 00:09:08,156
Where doyouwant to be?

207
00:09:09,636 --> 00:09:11,072
Here.

208
00:09:11,159 --> 00:09:12,639
'Glad to hear it.'

209
00:09:13,814 --> 00:09:14,902
Here's the case file.

210
00:09:16,251 --> 00:09:18,122
Good to have you aboard, Cho.

211
00:09:18,209 --> 00:09:21,125
[intense music]

212
00:09:28,002 --> 00:09:30,744
[phone ringing]

213
00:09:30,831 --> 00:09:33,921
Oh. Just admiring your son.
He as tall as you?

214
00:09:34,008 --> 00:09:36,097
No, no.
He's just nine years old.

215
00:09:36,184 --> 00:09:39,361
Right. So, what, he's
about, uh, Tork's size?

216
00:09:39,448 --> 00:09:42,364
[Niskin laughing]

217
00:09:45,236 --> 00:09:47,674
[laughing]
No, man. No,
Tork's not that short.

218
00:09:47,761 --> 00:09:49,893
No, my little man's probably
about, like, right here.

219
00:09:51,982 --> 00:09:54,855
[Niskin laughing]

220
00:10:06,040 --> 00:10:06,954
Hey.

221
00:10:08,825 --> 00:10:12,133
Was Niskin just
talking about me?

222
00:10:12,220 --> 00:10:14,396
Yeah, I-I gotta make
an important phone call.

223
00:10:16,572 --> 00:10:18,530
Son of a bitch.

224
00:10:21,664 --> 00:10:24,711
Hi. I need some help
locating a special item.

225
00:10:29,019 --> 00:10:31,413
Gym's owner is Jocelyn Chapin.

226
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:33,197
If you don't mind,
I'll take lead.

227
00:10:33,284 --> 00:10:35,896
- Lead. Sure.
- Good.

228
00:10:35,983 --> 00:10:36,897
Ms. Chapin?

229
00:10:38,333 --> 00:10:40,988
Ms. Chapin, hi.
Agent Ray Haffner, CBI.

230
00:10:41,075 --> 00:10:42,424
This is Patrick Jane.

231
00:10:42,511 --> 00:10:44,948
- He's a consultant.
- Hi.

232
00:10:45,035 --> 00:10:46,863
- I can't believe what happened.
- I understand.

233
00:10:46,950 --> 00:10:48,691
How long had
Kuzmenko worked here?

234
00:10:48,778 --> 00:10:51,999
Uh, almost nine years.

235
00:10:52,086 --> 00:10:54,305
He started here
when he got to the US.

236
00:10:54,392 --> 00:10:56,307
- He get along with people?
- Yeah, everybody.

237
00:10:56,394 --> 00:10:58,962
Markus was a real people person,
just charming.

238
00:10:59,049 --> 00:11:00,703
You couldn't say no to him.

239
00:11:00,790 --> 00:11:04,141
So was he having any
troubles recently or..

240
00:11:04,228 --> 00:11:06,187
Not that I know of. Uh..

241
00:11:06,274 --> 00:11:07,275
Um..

242
00:11:07,362 --> 00:11:08,842
[weights clanking]

243
00:11:08,929 --> 00:11:10,670
- Mike.
- Yeah?

244
00:11:10,757 --> 00:11:13,020
This-this is Mike Villanueva.
He and Markus were friends.

245
00:11:13,107 --> 00:11:14,717
These are, uh, agents from CBI.

246
00:11:14,804 --> 00:11:16,501
Oh. Oh, it's crazy, huh?

247
00:11:16,588 --> 00:11:18,025
Yeah, we're all
pretty racked up..

248
00:11:18,112 --> 00:11:21,158
Yeah, did Markus mention
any other difficulties

249
00:11:21,245 --> 00:11:23,378
that he was having,
money, other issues?

250
00:11:23,465 --> 00:11:26,120
No. No, he was doing great.

251
00:11:26,207 --> 00:11:30,167
Ooh. Feeling that. Yeah.

252
00:11:30,254 --> 00:11:33,214
- We boring you, Jane?
- Nope. Not at all.

253
00:11:33,301 --> 00:11:35,825
[grunts]

254
00:11:35,912 --> 00:11:37,566
[clanks]

255
00:11:37,653 --> 00:11:38,959
Sorry.

256
00:11:39,046 --> 00:11:42,005
I thought you were
taking lead on this.

257
00:11:42,092 --> 00:11:46,096
How many clients was
Kuzmenko sleeping with?

258
00:11:46,183 --> 00:11:48,969
- Excuse me?
- You said he was charming.

259
00:11:49,056 --> 00:11:52,102
Good-looker. He must've
had his pick of the bunch.

260
00:11:52,189 --> 00:11:55,802
I, uh, I gotta get
back to my client now.

261
00:11:55,889 --> 00:11:58,282
Uh, I have a firm
policy against that.

262
00:11:58,369 --> 00:11:59,762
Doesn't answer the question.

263
00:11:59,849 --> 00:12:01,546
Listen, a lot
of prominent people

264
00:12:01,633 --> 00:12:03,766
come here, alright?

265
00:12:03,853 --> 00:12:07,204
Politicians,
politicians' wives

266
00:12:07,291 --> 00:12:08,815
and frankly, they
expect some discretion.

267
00:12:08,902 --> 00:12:11,992
Yeah, we don't really care.
Answer his question.

268
00:12:12,079 --> 00:12:14,951
Yeah, I-I heard rumors about
Markus and some of his clients.

269
00:12:15,038 --> 00:12:16,518
If they were true, I would've

270
00:12:16,605 --> 00:12:18,825
had to fire him.
I didn't wanna do that.

271
00:12:18,912 --> 00:12:22,045
Markus was the most
successful trainer that we had

272
00:12:22,132 --> 00:12:24,656
so I-I didn't ask.

273
00:12:24,744 --> 00:12:26,528
I don't have any names.

274
00:12:26,615 --> 00:12:27,790
Except your own.

275
00:12:27,877 --> 00:12:29,879
Yeah.

276
00:12:29,966 --> 00:12:31,272
Yeah, a long time ago

277
00:12:31,359 --> 00:12:34,057
very, very briefly,
Markus and I had a thing.

278
00:12:34,144 --> 00:12:35,755
A thing?

279
00:12:35,842 --> 00:12:38,627
Nothing serious.

280
00:12:38,714 --> 00:12:42,892
- We were friends.
- Oh. Okay.

281
00:12:42,979 --> 00:12:45,460
I'm gonna need the names
of all of Kuzmenko's clients.

282
00:12:56,732 --> 00:12:59,648
[instrumental music]

283
00:13:01,084 --> 00:13:02,216
Hi.

284
00:13:03,608 --> 00:13:05,088
Lovely rings you have.

285
00:13:05,175 --> 00:13:06,568
Thanks.

286
00:13:06,655 --> 00:13:08,178
Expensive, I expect.

287
00:13:08,265 --> 00:13:10,093
Your husband must
be very generous.

288
00:13:10,180 --> 00:13:12,095
Yeah. Do you mind?

289
00:13:15,316 --> 00:13:17,274
Just, uh, one more thing.

290
00:13:17,361 --> 00:13:19,015
Markus Kuzmenko

291
00:13:19,102 --> 00:13:21,626
more of a gentle lover
or a brute?

292
00:13:21,713 --> 00:13:23,541
[woman grunts]

293
00:13:23,628 --> 00:13:26,066
[female #1]
'Oh! Are you alright?'

294
00:13:26,153 --> 00:13:27,894
- You okay?
- Man down!

295
00:13:27,981 --> 00:13:29,678
[door opening]

296
00:13:30,505 --> 00:13:31,811
[door closes]

297
00:13:37,686 --> 00:13:39,427
Your Mr. Jane,
how did he know?

298
00:13:39,514 --> 00:13:42,082
Your eyes were puffy.
He could tell you'd been crying.

299
00:13:42,169 --> 00:13:44,867
Does my husband have
to find out about this?

300
00:13:44,954 --> 00:13:46,303
Depends on what you say.

301
00:13:46,390 --> 00:13:48,088
[sighs]

302
00:13:49,916 --> 00:13:52,266
It happened
a few months back.

303
00:13:52,353 --> 00:13:55,573
Markus was coming
by my house once a week

304
00:13:55,660 --> 00:13:58,054
to work on my abs.

305
00:13:58,141 --> 00:14:00,317
He was so funny and charming

306
00:14:00,404 --> 00:14:03,668
with a faintly...wicked quality.

307
00:14:05,192 --> 00:14:07,759
Amoral, you know what I mean?

308
00:14:07,847 --> 00:14:09,718
Unaware of moral precepts

309
00:14:09,805 --> 00:14:11,241
as opposed
to being purposely bad.

310
00:14:11,328 --> 00:14:13,113
'It can be a very
attractive quality.'

311
00:14:14,288 --> 00:14:16,638
My husband's not like that.

312
00:14:16,725 --> 00:14:19,249
Amoral, I mean.

313
00:14:19,336 --> 00:14:23,166
Markus never hit on me.
I think I seduced him.

314
00:14:23,253 --> 00:14:25,125
How long did
the relationship last?

315
00:14:26,822 --> 00:14:29,216
Twice.

316
00:14:29,303 --> 00:14:30,913
Markus wasn't a relationship.

317
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,698
If it was a just a fling
a couple months back

318
00:14:33,785 --> 00:14:35,918
why were you crying
this morning?

319
00:14:36,005 --> 00:14:38,616
He's dead.

320
00:14:38,703 --> 00:14:40,053
It's sad.

321
00:14:42,794 --> 00:14:45,754
Your phone records show
Markus called you last week.

322
00:14:45,841 --> 00:14:47,538
'You sure
the relationship was over?'

323
00:14:47,625 --> 00:14:49,410
Um..

324
00:14:49,497 --> 00:14:51,499
...Markus said that
he had a business venture

325
00:14:51,586 --> 00:14:55,068
he wanted to talk to my husband
and me about investing in.

326
00:14:55,155 --> 00:14:56,852
[sighs]
It didn't occur to him that

327
00:14:56,939 --> 00:14:58,549
that would be
an awkward conversation.

328
00:14:58,636 --> 00:14:59,986
What was the business?

329
00:15:00,073 --> 00:15:01,291
I don't know.

330
00:15:01,378 --> 00:15:03,293
I told him
we couldn't discuss it.

331
00:15:06,035 --> 00:15:09,256
[sighs]
So what about my husband?

332
00:15:09,343 --> 00:15:12,302
There's a pretty good chance
we won't need to talk to him.

333
00:15:12,389 --> 00:15:15,088
I'd hate for him
to have to find out

334
00:15:15,175 --> 00:15:17,133
although a little bit,
I wish he would.

335
00:15:19,440 --> 00:15:22,530
- Should we keep an eye on her?
- She seemed sincere to me.

336
00:15:22,617 --> 00:15:24,967
Eh, she's a timid
suburban adulteress.

337
00:15:25,054 --> 00:15:26,490
The only thing
she's killed lately

338
00:15:26,577 --> 00:15:27,883
is a bottle of chardonnay.

339
00:15:27,970 --> 00:15:29,058
[indistinct chattering on radio]

340
00:15:29,145 --> 00:15:32,540
Niskin! You think this is funny?

341
00:15:32,627 --> 00:15:34,324
What do you mean?

342
00:15:34,411 --> 00:15:37,371
I just found these
on my desk with a note.

343
00:15:37,458 --> 00:15:40,200
"Tork, try theselifts."
That funny to you?

344
00:15:40,287 --> 00:15:42,506
[laughing]
I got nothing
to do with that, man.

345
00:15:42,593 --> 00:15:44,378
I wear lifts,
so I'm a joke?

346
00:15:44,465 --> 00:15:46,162
I don't know
about your lifts

347
00:15:46,249 --> 00:15:48,773
but, yes, these are
real funny, man.

348
00:15:48,860 --> 00:15:50,688
[both grunting]

349
00:15:50,775 --> 00:15:52,473
[shattering]

350
00:15:52,560 --> 00:15:54,083
[both grunting]

351
00:15:54,170 --> 00:15:55,955
- Come on, come on.
- Help.

352
00:15:56,042 --> 00:15:58,261
- Break it up!
- My money's on the little guy.

353
00:16:04,267 --> 00:16:07,792
I was engaged
to this guy, Craig.

354
00:16:07,879 --> 00:16:10,143
You know, he seemed
like a good person.

355
00:16:10,230 --> 00:16:11,753
We were happy.

356
00:16:13,146 --> 00:16:15,322
Then I found out
that he was an assassin

357
00:16:15,409 --> 00:16:18,412
for a serial killer
named Red John

358
00:16:18,499 --> 00:16:21,676
and he tried to...kill me

359
00:16:21,763 --> 00:16:23,895
and some other people, so..

360
00:16:23,983 --> 00:16:26,333
I had to shoot him.

361
00:16:26,420 --> 00:16:29,945
Tell me, how it does
it feel telling that

362
00:16:30,032 --> 00:16:32,600
recounting it?

363
00:16:32,687 --> 00:16:35,777
Not good. Pretty bad.

364
00:16:35,864 --> 00:16:38,258
Can you elaborate?

365
00:16:38,345 --> 00:16:41,043
Can I ask, how long does
this therapy usually last?

366
00:16:41,130 --> 00:16:43,306
'It depends.
There's no set calendar.'

367
00:16:44,699 --> 00:16:47,571
I'm sensing
hesitation on your part.

368
00:16:47,658 --> 00:16:50,139
I guess..

369
00:16:50,226 --> 00:16:52,924
...I just don't believe
that this will really work.

370
00:16:53,012 --> 00:16:54,926
Talking about it.

371
00:16:55,014 --> 00:16:57,320
What's the point?

372
00:16:57,407 --> 00:17:01,237
Grace, somebody you trusted
and loved betrayed you

373
00:17:01,324 --> 00:17:04,719
betrayed you so badly,
you had to take his life.

374
00:17:04,806 --> 00:17:06,590
That needs to be talked about.

375
00:17:08,375 --> 00:17:10,638
[chuckles]
It could've been worse.

376
00:17:10,725 --> 00:17:13,380
I could've married him.

377
00:17:13,467 --> 00:17:16,426
[cell phone ringing]

378
00:17:16,513 --> 00:17:18,776
No phone calls during session.
That's a rule.

379
00:17:18,863 --> 00:17:22,258
It's work. I gotta take this.

380
00:17:22,345 --> 00:17:24,304
Sorry.

381
00:17:24,391 --> 00:17:26,828
Van Pelt.

382
00:17:26,915 --> 00:17:30,092
I can be there in 15 minutes.
Who should I report to?

383
00:17:30,179 --> 00:17:32,921
Since Agent Tork has been
removed from active duty

384
00:17:33,008 --> 00:17:35,793
I assumed you would
want to replace him.

385
00:17:35,880 --> 00:17:37,708
Was I incorrect?

386
00:17:38,970 --> 00:17:41,625
[instrumental music]

387
00:17:43,453 --> 00:17:45,934
- Agent?
- No, you were not.

388
00:17:46,021 --> 00:17:48,937
We could, uh, definitely
use another set of hands.

389
00:17:49,024 --> 00:17:51,592
- Go in. We'll put you to work.
- Thank you, sir.

390
00:17:51,679 --> 00:17:53,637
Alright.

391
00:17:58,599 --> 00:18:01,080
Everything alright, agent?

392
00:18:02,342 --> 00:18:04,213
Oh, yeah. Excuse me.

393
00:18:11,177 --> 00:18:13,353
That was a nice game
you ran on Tork.

394
00:18:13,440 --> 00:18:16,095
Hmm. Tork.

395
00:18:16,182 --> 00:18:18,706
He was angry with
everybody taller than him.

396
00:18:18,793 --> 00:18:20,577
Certain to burst eventually.

397
00:18:20,664 --> 00:18:22,753
You wanted my attention.

398
00:18:22,840 --> 00:18:24,712
You got my attention.

399
00:18:24,799 --> 00:18:28,455
But if you try to run
another game on me or my people

400
00:18:28,542 --> 00:18:29,978
we're gonna have a problem.

401
00:18:30,065 --> 00:18:33,155
You know that Babe Ruth
wore a cabbage leaf

402
00:18:33,242 --> 00:18:36,071
under his cap
to keep himself cool?

403
00:18:36,158 --> 00:18:37,899
Changed it
every other inning.

404
00:18:37,986 --> 00:18:39,770
- Agent?
- Yeah.

405
00:18:39,857 --> 00:18:42,077
The night before the murder,
a woman called in

406
00:18:42,164 --> 00:18:44,384
a suspicious man in a car
near Kuzmenko's house.

407
00:18:44,471 --> 00:18:47,300
- Said he'd been there a while.
- She get the plate?

408
00:18:47,387 --> 00:18:49,171
Belongs to Mike Villanueva.

409
00:18:49,258 --> 00:18:52,261
Mike, when we talked,
you didn't mention visiting

410
00:18:52,348 --> 00:18:54,568
your buddy Markus' house
right before he got killed.

411
00:18:54,655 --> 00:18:57,310
I didn't wanna talk about
it in front of my boss.

412
00:18:57,397 --> 00:18:59,877
Why are you worried
about your boss?

413
00:18:59,964 --> 00:19:02,271
Business is down at the gym.

414
00:19:02,358 --> 00:19:05,013
Jocelyn's on us
to bring in new clients.

415
00:19:05,100 --> 00:19:07,581
Anyone who loses
clients is in trouble.

416
00:19:07,668 --> 00:19:09,409
And you're losing clients.

417
00:19:09,496 --> 00:19:10,453
No.

418
00:19:10,540 --> 00:19:12,412
[chuckles]
No, I'm not.

419
00:19:12,499 --> 00:19:14,153
Was Markus stealing
your clients?

420
00:19:14,240 --> 00:19:17,678
Yeah. Basically.

421
00:19:17,765 --> 00:19:20,637
Look, Markus got
the looks and the talk

422
00:19:20,724 --> 00:19:23,684
so people wanna
work with him.

423
00:19:23,771 --> 00:19:26,469
He doesn't push
them like I do.

424
00:19:26,556 --> 00:19:29,603
I work 'em hard.
And you know what I get?

425
00:19:29,690 --> 00:19:31,866
Five clients signing up
with Markus in a month.

426
00:19:31,953 --> 00:19:33,998
You went to Markus'
house to confront him.

427
00:19:34,085 --> 00:19:36,523
- I had to have it out with him.
- What happened?

428
00:19:36,610 --> 00:19:38,177
'Nothing. He apologized.'

429
00:19:38,264 --> 00:19:40,483
He took the clients
'cause he needed the cash.

430
00:19:40,570 --> 00:19:43,269
But he'd gotten money. He didn't
need the extra clients anymore.

431
00:19:43,356 --> 00:19:45,532
Then we went into his house
and got wasted.

432
00:19:45,619 --> 00:19:47,838
[chuckles]
And that's Markus, man.

433
00:19:47,925 --> 00:19:49,884
Went there to punch him,
ended up partying.

434
00:19:49,971 --> 00:19:52,452
- What'd he need the money for?
- He wouldn't tell me.

435
00:19:52,539 --> 00:19:54,541
Said that nobody
at the gym could know.

436
00:19:54,628 --> 00:19:56,369
Where'd he get the cash?

437
00:19:56,456 --> 00:19:58,197
He wouldn't tell me.

438
00:19:58,284 --> 00:19:59,633
But, uh..

439
00:19:59,720 --> 00:20:01,200
...I got a feeling..

440
00:20:01,287 --> 00:20:03,593
...from the way he talked..

441
00:20:03,680 --> 00:20:05,291
...it wasn't anyplace good.

442
00:20:07,075 --> 00:20:10,905
The question is, what did
Kuzmenko need money for?

443
00:20:10,992 --> 00:20:13,560
- And who did he get it from?
- That's two questions.

444
00:20:13,647 --> 00:20:16,693
- Excuse me?
- Two questions.

445
00:20:16,780 --> 00:20:19,043
Kuzmenko's boss at the gym,
Jocelyn Chapin

446
00:20:19,130 --> 00:20:20,828
sent over a list of his clients.

447
00:20:20,915 --> 00:20:23,004
There are some pretty
high-powered people on it.

448
00:20:23,091 --> 00:20:26,137
State senator, assemblyman,
people working for the governor.

449
00:20:26,225 --> 00:20:29,358
- It's the names I recognized.
- There's a lot of women here.

450
00:20:29,445 --> 00:20:32,709
[Haffner]
'Some of them would pay to keep
an affair with Kuzmenko quiet.'

451
00:20:32,796 --> 00:20:35,234
Think Kuzmenko slept with one
of them then blackmailed her.

452
00:20:35,321 --> 00:20:38,715
Maybe. What do you think, Jane?

453
00:20:38,802 --> 00:20:40,456
Mmm..

454
00:20:40,543 --> 00:20:43,242
Well, I wanna pursue it.
Look for the money Kuzmenko got.

455
00:20:43,329 --> 00:20:45,766
If it's not in his bank account,
check other accounts

456
00:20:45,853 --> 00:20:46,767
'safety deposit boxes..'

457
00:20:46,854 --> 00:20:48,116
Okay.

458
00:20:48,203 --> 00:20:49,639
Cho, we'll work
through this list

459
00:20:49,726 --> 00:20:51,206
starting with
his most recent clients

460
00:20:51,293 --> 00:20:53,164
try identifying
who he was sleeping with.

461
00:20:53,252 --> 00:20:55,819
Where are you going?

462
00:20:55,906 --> 00:20:58,344
Uh, I thought I'd just go
over to Kuzmenko's house

463
00:20:58,431 --> 00:20:59,997
take a little look around.

464
00:21:00,084 --> 00:21:04,001
- Why?
- It's what I do.

465
00:21:04,088 --> 00:21:07,091
Not alone.
Masterson, go with him.

466
00:21:07,178 --> 00:21:08,615
- Okay.
- Keep an eye on him.

467
00:21:08,702 --> 00:21:09,703
Yep.

468
00:21:11,835 --> 00:21:12,923
Okeydokey.

469
00:21:21,367 --> 00:21:23,238
So what are you
looking for?

470
00:21:23,325 --> 00:21:25,196
[Jane]
'Whatever's here to see.'

471
00:21:25,284 --> 00:21:26,981
[Masterson]
'We already went
through the house.'

472
00:21:27,068 --> 00:21:29,592
- 'We didn't find anything.'
- I'm sure you didn't.

473
00:21:33,988 --> 00:21:37,252
Pretty dull, yeah?

474
00:21:37,339 --> 00:21:39,776
If you wanna get a coffee,
I won't tell Haffner.

475
00:21:39,863 --> 00:21:41,300
Yeah, I'm gonna go do that.

476
00:21:41,387 --> 00:21:42,431
[scoffs]

477
00:21:43,693 --> 00:21:46,783
Suit yourself.

478
00:21:46,870 --> 00:21:49,830
Some more of this
tottering around.

479
00:21:53,964 --> 00:21:55,836
'See, me...uh..'

480
00:21:57,446 --> 00:21:59,579
...I like to take
a break now and then.

481
00:22:05,411 --> 00:22:09,066
A moment to, uh, rest and relax.

482
00:22:10,546 --> 00:22:13,549
Take it easy.

483
00:22:13,636 --> 00:22:16,683
Close the eyes..

484
00:22:16,770 --> 00:22:20,164
...and shut out the day.

485
00:22:20,251 --> 00:22:22,428
'Rest..'

486
00:22:22,515 --> 00:22:23,820
'...and relax.'

487
00:22:25,256 --> 00:22:28,172
Try not to see anything..

488
00:22:28,259 --> 00:22:30,174
...or hear anything.

489
00:22:31,567 --> 00:22:33,787
'Let time stop.'

490
00:22:33,874 --> 00:22:35,136
'Rest..'

491
00:22:36,659 --> 00:22:38,531
...and relax.

492
00:22:40,663 --> 00:22:43,536
[instrumental music]

493
00:22:48,758 --> 00:22:49,846
[thud]

494
00:22:51,065 --> 00:22:53,023
- What happened?
- Huh? Nothing.

495
00:22:56,462 --> 00:22:58,333
You did something to me,
didn't you?

496
00:23:00,291 --> 00:23:01,858
You hypnotized me.

497
00:23:01,945 --> 00:23:03,643
[chuckles]

498
00:23:03,730 --> 00:23:05,645
That's absurd.

499
00:23:05,732 --> 00:23:07,255
You know that only weak-minded

500
00:23:07,342 --> 00:23:11,041
and easily manipulated
people can be hypnotized.

501
00:23:11,128 --> 00:23:12,956
Yeah, we're leaving.

502
00:23:13,043 --> 00:23:15,132
- I'm not finished.
- Yes, you are.

503
00:23:15,219 --> 00:23:16,395
Okay.

504
00:23:16,482 --> 00:23:19,398
[instrumental music]

505
00:23:24,446 --> 00:23:27,362
[intense music]

506
00:23:35,109 --> 00:23:37,067
[beeping]

507
00:23:37,154 --> 00:23:39,374
[phone rings]

508
00:23:39,461 --> 00:23:40,636
- 'Hello?'
- Laroche.

509
00:23:40,723 --> 00:23:42,464
- 'Yes.'
- Hi. It's Lisbon.

510
00:23:42,551 --> 00:23:44,945
I wanted to ask a favor.

511
00:23:45,032 --> 00:23:46,425
It's about Sally Carter.

512
00:23:48,035 --> 00:23:49,428
Sir?

513
00:23:49,515 --> 00:23:50,864
I'm busy, agent.

514
00:23:50,951 --> 00:23:52,561
Sorry, but there's
nothing unusual

515
00:23:52,648 --> 00:23:54,824
in Kuzmenko's checking
or savings accounts.

516
00:23:54,911 --> 00:23:56,870
- I found something else though.
- 'What?'

517
00:23:56,957 --> 00:23:59,307
A couple days before he
was killed, Kuzmenko made

518
00:23:59,394 --> 00:24:01,831
four phone calls
to an Oleg Mostovoi.

519
00:24:01,918 --> 00:24:04,355
He's not with the gym,
and it's the only time

520
00:24:04,443 --> 00:24:05,966
Kuzmenko made the calls.

521
00:24:06,053 --> 00:24:08,011
It's right before Kuzmenko
started saying he had

522
00:24:08,098 --> 00:24:10,927
a lotta cash. Maybe Mostovoi
had something to do with it.

523
00:24:11,014 --> 00:24:13,974
Or maybe he's a friend. Wanna
find the money, track the money.

524
00:24:14,061 --> 00:24:16,106
Find that, then we
can talk about Mustavoi

525
00:24:16,193 --> 00:24:19,109
Muscavoi, whatever his name is.

526
00:24:19,196 --> 00:24:21,068
[cell phone ringing]

527
00:24:22,983 --> 00:24:24,593
- Van Pelt.
- 'Grace.'

528
00:24:24,680 --> 00:24:27,291
- 'Time for the secret meeting.'
- I'll be right there.

529
00:24:27,378 --> 00:24:30,643
The way I see it, we got one
shot at saving Lisbon's job.

530
00:24:30,730 --> 00:24:32,471
[Rigsby]
You think we can?

531
00:24:32,558 --> 00:24:35,952
All we have to do is solve
the murder without Haffner.

532
00:24:36,039 --> 00:24:37,824
Then we take
the murderer to Bertram

533
00:24:37,911 --> 00:24:40,087
use our success as leverage
to get Lisbon back.

534
00:24:40,174 --> 00:24:42,219
- That's not gonna work.
- Come on.

535
00:24:42,306 --> 00:24:44,483
- When have I steered you wrong?
- Recently?

536
00:24:44,570 --> 00:24:46,746
How do we solve
the murder without Haffner?

537
00:24:46,833 --> 00:24:49,226
We catch the killer
before he does.

538
00:24:49,313 --> 00:24:51,098
'Kuzmenko was an athlete.'

539
00:24:51,185 --> 00:24:53,535
He was a compulsive
record keeper.

540
00:24:53,622 --> 00:24:56,320
No doubt that included
very detailed records

541
00:24:56,407 --> 00:25:00,107
of his sexual partners...
probably with a rating system.

542
00:25:00,194 --> 00:25:01,717
'You gotta get into the house'

543
00:25:01,804 --> 00:25:04,807
and you have to find
that list of lovers.

544
00:25:04,894 --> 00:25:06,722
It's probably in an address book

545
00:25:06,809 --> 00:25:08,942
a calendar or a journal.

546
00:25:09,029 --> 00:25:11,118
I don't like it.
It's insubordination.

547
00:25:11,205 --> 00:25:14,251
- 'We're already on the edge.'
- It's for the boss.

548
00:25:14,338 --> 00:25:17,254
This goes wrong, we're out. They
won't give us another chance.

549
00:25:17,341 --> 00:25:18,952
I don't care. I'm in.

550
00:25:21,607 --> 00:25:23,043
Me too.

551
00:25:23,130 --> 00:25:26,002
[intense music]

552
00:25:26,089 --> 00:25:27,569
Okay.

553
00:25:27,656 --> 00:25:29,179
That's the spirit.

554
00:25:29,266 --> 00:25:32,182
[intense music]

555
00:25:35,098 --> 00:25:37,971
[indistinct chattering]

556
00:25:46,240 --> 00:25:48,416
You wanted to know
if Jane is doing anything.

557
00:25:48,503 --> 00:25:51,288
[music continues]

558
00:25:51,375 --> 00:25:53,247
Take a seat.

559
00:26:03,605 --> 00:26:05,651
[instrumental music]

560
00:26:07,087 --> 00:26:08,828
Thank you for doing this.

561
00:26:08,915 --> 00:26:11,004
I don't mind, Teresa.

562
00:26:11,091 --> 00:26:13,223
Bertram has treated you poorly.

563
00:26:13,310 --> 00:26:15,965
Not that he solicits
my opinion on such matters.

564
00:26:18,141 --> 00:26:20,840
Why do you wanna
talk to Sally Carter?

565
00:26:20,927 --> 00:26:24,365
I wanna ask her some
questions about Red John.

566
00:26:24,452 --> 00:26:26,062
Hmm.

567
00:26:26,149 --> 00:26:28,891
She hasn't spoken since
her arrest, not to anyone

568
00:26:28,978 --> 00:26:31,720
so set your
expectations accordingly.

569
00:26:31,807 --> 00:26:34,505
Just so you know, um..

570
00:26:34,593 --> 00:26:37,073
...I am leaving
the Major Crimes Unit

571
00:26:37,160 --> 00:26:39,554
returning to
professional services.

572
00:26:39,641 --> 00:26:41,817
Was always
a temporary assignment.

573
00:26:41,904 --> 00:26:44,777
Well, good luck to you.

574
00:26:44,864 --> 00:26:46,953
Thank you.

575
00:26:47,040 --> 00:26:50,783
It was...interesting..

576
00:26:50,870 --> 00:26:53,437
...working with
you and Jane.

577
00:26:53,524 --> 00:26:56,702
I think that's the polite word
for what it was, but thanks.

578
00:27:04,144 --> 00:27:05,145
Open it up.

579
00:27:05,232 --> 00:27:06,363
[clanks]

580
00:27:07,147 --> 00:27:09,453
Mrs. Carter.

581
00:27:09,540 --> 00:27:10,846
'Mrs. Carter.'

582
00:27:15,329 --> 00:27:17,418
Get a medical team here!

583
00:27:17,505 --> 00:27:18,898
[woman shouting indistinctly]

584
00:27:20,813 --> 00:27:23,816
[woman on PA]
'Medical team
to cell C-38.'

585
00:27:23,903 --> 00:27:26,209
'Medical team
to cell C-3-8.'

586
00:27:26,296 --> 00:27:27,776
[alarm blaring]

587
00:27:27,863 --> 00:27:30,039
"All prisoners,
return to your cells.'

588
00:27:30,126 --> 00:27:32,999
[blaring continues]

589
00:27:35,088 --> 00:27:36,350
How?

590
00:27:36,437 --> 00:27:37,960
She got ahold of a spoon

591
00:27:38,047 --> 00:27:41,834
sharpened the shaft
slit her wrists and throat.

592
00:27:41,921 --> 00:27:43,270
What are you doing here?

593
00:27:43,357 --> 00:27:45,838
Came to ask her if her
husband was Red John.

594
00:27:45,925 --> 00:27:48,014
- Because you need convincing.
- Nice try.

595
00:27:48,101 --> 00:27:51,582
Turns out I didn't need to ask.
She left a note.

596
00:27:51,670 --> 00:27:53,410
"My life began the day I met

597
00:27:53,497 --> 00:27:56,065
"the Lord and master
of my heart, Red John.

598
00:27:56,152 --> 00:27:59,808
Now that he is dead,
all is shadows and silence."

599
00:27:59,895 --> 00:28:01,767
She goes on.

600
00:28:01,854 --> 00:28:03,812
Yeah, it's a bit purple.
Let me see.

601
00:28:03,899 --> 00:28:05,553
[intense music]

602
00:28:07,511 --> 00:28:10,036
See?

603
00:28:10,123 --> 00:28:11,080
You did it.

604
00:28:11,167 --> 00:28:13,343
You killed Red John.

605
00:28:13,430 --> 00:28:15,345
- Jane--
- Shh! I'm reading.

606
00:28:15,432 --> 00:28:18,305
[music continues]

607
00:28:20,002 --> 00:28:21,134
[muttering indistinctly]

608
00:28:22,309 --> 00:28:24,920
So how was the therapist?

609
00:28:25,007 --> 00:28:27,183
Talking about what happened
just makes me feel worse.

610
00:28:27,270 --> 00:28:28,750
I'm not gonna
see her anymore.

611
00:28:28,837 --> 00:28:30,752
Grace, you need counseling.
The job says so.

612
00:28:30,839 --> 00:28:32,580
It could be
a spiritual adviser.

613
00:28:32,667 --> 00:28:34,625
You know, my godfather's
a Presbyterian minister.

614
00:28:34,713 --> 00:28:37,759
- He'll sign the forms for me.
- You think that's a good idea?

615
00:28:37,846 --> 00:28:39,195
Yeah, I do.

616
00:28:42,242 --> 00:28:43,417
I got this.

617
00:28:43,504 --> 00:28:46,202
[instrumental music]

618
00:28:46,289 --> 00:28:47,595
[rattling]

619
00:28:49,902 --> 00:28:50,903
Agents?

620
00:28:52,861 --> 00:28:54,210
Looking for this?

621
00:28:55,864 --> 00:28:58,824
So I found this hidden
in Kuzmenko's bedroom.

622
00:28:58,911 --> 00:29:02,175
Fourteen of his clients are
named in it, with sexual details

623
00:29:02,262 --> 00:29:04,917
'and some kind of
coded rating system.'

624
00:29:05,004 --> 00:29:06,309
There's a really good chance

625
00:29:06,396 --> 00:29:08,442
that one of these
women killed him.

626
00:29:08,529 --> 00:29:10,705
But you weren't gonna
tell me that, were you?

627
00:29:10,792 --> 00:29:12,663
Probably not. No.

628
00:29:12,751 --> 00:29:14,056
Right.

629
00:29:14,143 --> 00:29:16,972
You wanted to solve
this one yourself.

630
00:29:17,059 --> 00:29:20,759
Uh...that was the plan.

631
00:29:20,846 --> 00:29:23,805
[chuckles]
Did you really think
that was gonna happen?

632
00:29:23,892 --> 00:29:27,374
Like I wasn't watching
you every minute?

633
00:29:27,461 --> 00:29:29,158
Jane, I would have
worked with you

634
00:29:29,245 --> 00:29:31,117
if you'd given me the chance.

635
00:29:34,947 --> 00:29:37,427
I'm taking this to Bertram.
Get out.

636
00:29:44,130 --> 00:29:46,045
- He go for it?
- Oh, did he.

637
00:29:46,132 --> 00:29:48,047
Time to break the news.

638
00:29:48,134 --> 00:29:50,310
[instrumental music]

639
00:29:51,398 --> 00:29:53,530
Let me get this straight.

640
00:29:53,617 --> 00:29:56,055
Haffner has the address book,
but it's a fake?

641
00:29:56,142 --> 00:29:57,883
Fake as a three dollar bill.

642
00:29:57,970 --> 00:30:00,494
Then how'd he find it
in Kuzmenko's house?

643
00:30:00,581 --> 00:30:02,061
Well, I copied the names

644
00:30:02,148 --> 00:30:04,237
from the list of
Kuzmenko's clients.

645
00:30:04,324 --> 00:30:07,066
Then I put the book
where Haffner would find it.

646
00:30:13,507 --> 00:30:16,466
I told Haffner Jane thought the
address book was at the house.

647
00:30:16,553 --> 00:30:18,251
- So he went to the house.
- Why?

648
00:30:18,338 --> 00:30:19,426
Haffner's all
about surveillance.

649
00:30:19,513 --> 00:30:21,210
I knew he'd be following us.

650
00:30:21,297 --> 00:30:23,517
I had to do something
to throw him off.

651
00:30:23,604 --> 00:30:26,041
Now we can solve the case
without Haffner watching us.

652
00:30:26,128 --> 00:30:28,087
- No. No, no.
- Don't be hasty.

653
00:30:28,174 --> 00:30:31,351
I will deal
with you in a minute!

654
00:30:31,438 --> 00:30:33,396
When Bertram finds out,
he'll have a coronary.

655
00:30:33,483 --> 00:30:34,963
We knew there was a risk.

656
00:30:35,050 --> 00:30:36,965
Which is why you
shouldn't have done it!

657
00:30:39,098 --> 00:30:40,882
'Go to Haffner and apologize.'

658
00:30:40,969 --> 00:30:43,493
- Tell him it was Jane's fault.
- That hardly seems fair.

659
00:30:43,580 --> 00:30:46,018
Maybe you can convince him
to give you another chance.

660
00:30:46,105 --> 00:30:49,108
- It's not gonna happen.
- She's right.

661
00:30:49,195 --> 00:30:53,286
Oh, God. Why are you doing this?

662
00:30:53,373 --> 00:30:56,593
You were okay. All you had
to do was keep your heads down.

663
00:30:56,680 --> 00:30:58,595
We wanna work for you.

664
00:30:58,682 --> 00:31:01,598
[intense music]

665
00:31:07,517 --> 00:31:09,215
Let me talk to Jane alone.

666
00:31:18,224 --> 00:31:20,356
I could kill you
with my bare hands.

667
00:31:20,443 --> 00:31:21,967
Well, it's not
the first time.

668
00:31:22,054 --> 00:31:23,794
Come on.
You don't have a choice.

669
00:31:23,882 --> 00:31:25,579
You do nothing,
they get fired.

670
00:31:25,666 --> 00:31:28,451
You work with me,
they keep their jobs.

671
00:31:28,538 --> 00:31:30,323
And you might even
get yours back.

672
00:31:30,410 --> 00:31:32,281
Alright, but it's
just you and me.

673
00:31:32,368 --> 00:31:35,154
They don't do anything else.

674
00:31:35,241 --> 00:31:36,720
What's the plan?

675
00:31:36,807 --> 00:31:40,420
Plan is...
we catch the killer.

676
00:31:40,507 --> 00:31:41,900
Kind of obvious.

677
00:31:43,075 --> 00:31:44,554
Jocelyn Chapin.
Patrick Jane.

678
00:31:44,641 --> 00:31:46,600
We're on the verge
of catching the killer

679
00:31:46,687 --> 00:31:48,907
but we'll need your
help to pin him down.

680
00:31:48,994 --> 00:31:52,214
Get a pen and paper. You gotta
get some people to your gym.

681
00:31:52,301 --> 00:31:53,912
We'll need
some of the, shh

682
00:31:53,999 --> 00:31:57,132
the-the glowy spray stuff
the crime scene guys use.

683
00:31:57,219 --> 00:31:59,134
- Glowy spray stuff?
- Yes, the glowy spray stuff.

684
00:31:59,221 --> 00:32:00,962
It's vital.
Got a pen?

685
00:32:06,098 --> 00:32:07,664
We appreciate your help.

686
00:32:07,751 --> 00:32:09,275
Think this will work?

687
00:32:09,362 --> 00:32:11,494
Oh, I'm about 68.2% sure.

688
00:32:13,061 --> 00:32:15,150
Can I have everybody's
attention, please?

689
00:32:15,237 --> 00:32:18,501
Yes, everybody, you, those
with the, uh, ear things.

690
00:32:18,588 --> 00:32:22,070
Uh, yes, could you,
thank you. Sir.

691
00:32:22,157 --> 00:32:24,333
You too. Thank you.
This won't hurt a bit.

692
00:32:24,420 --> 00:32:26,945
My name is Patrick Jane.
This is Teresa Lisbon.

693
00:32:27,032 --> 00:32:29,730
We're investigating
the death of Markus Kuzmenko.

694
00:32:29,817 --> 00:32:33,690
'His killer is somewhere
in this room right now.'

695
00:32:33,777 --> 00:32:36,215
Kuzmenko was struck in
the head with a heavy object.

696
00:32:36,302 --> 00:32:38,347
We believe it was
one of those weights

697
00:32:38,434 --> 00:32:42,221
'from this gym, so we're
gonna test them with a spray'

698
00:32:42,308 --> 00:32:45,920
that can detect trace elements
of blood on a surface by glowing

699
00:32:46,007 --> 00:32:47,574
'even after it's been cleaned.'

700
00:32:47,661 --> 00:32:50,359
We find the weight,
we find our murderer.

701
00:32:50,446 --> 00:32:53,884
So if you could all
sit tight, be patient

702
00:32:53,972 --> 00:32:56,713
the killer will soon
be revealed. Thank you.

703
00:32:56,800 --> 00:32:58,280
- 'Excuse me.'
- Yes.

704
00:32:58,367 --> 00:33:00,587
- Hi. I-I'm an attorney.
- Oh.

705
00:33:00,674 --> 00:33:03,851
Even if you do find blood
on one of those weights

706
00:33:03,938 --> 00:33:06,636
there's no way to connect it
to anyone in this room.

707
00:33:06,723 --> 00:33:08,856
- Ah, fingerprints, my friend.
- 'Fingerprints?'

708
00:33:08,943 --> 00:33:10,989
My fingerprints are
on lots of those weights.

709
00:33:11,076 --> 00:33:13,774
That doesn't mean I
used them to kill anybody.

710
00:33:13,861 --> 00:33:15,471
You make an interesting point.

711
00:33:15,558 --> 00:33:18,170
Okay. Hypothetical.

712
00:33:18,257 --> 00:33:21,869
What if I hid
the weight in my car?

713
00:33:21,956 --> 00:33:24,959
Or my office? My house?

714
00:33:25,046 --> 00:33:26,743
Would that prove
I was the killer?

715
00:33:26,830 --> 00:33:30,791
I-if you found the murder
weapon in my car, yeah.

716
00:33:30,878 --> 00:33:32,619
- Is it in your car?
- No.

717
00:33:32,706 --> 00:33:35,926
No, I knew it wasn't in your
car, because it's in her car.

718
00:33:36,014 --> 00:33:37,928
- 'Right, Jocelyn?'
- What?

719
00:33:38,016 --> 00:33:41,715
The weight you
used to kill Markus.

720
00:33:41,802 --> 00:33:45,240
You hid it in your car when we
told you we were coming over.

721
00:33:45,327 --> 00:33:47,155
- No.
- Oh, please.

722
00:33:47,242 --> 00:33:49,070
You practically
jumped out of your skin

723
00:33:49,157 --> 00:33:50,941
at the mention of "car."

724
00:33:51,029 --> 00:33:52,465
- No, I didn't.
- 'Yes, you did.'

725
00:33:52,552 --> 00:33:54,771
- No, I didn't.
- Yes, you did.

726
00:33:54,858 --> 00:33:57,209
Okay. There's only one
way we can settle this.

727
00:33:57,296 --> 00:33:58,601
Shall we?

728
00:34:05,391 --> 00:34:08,437
What's this?

729
00:34:08,524 --> 00:34:10,526
It's a kettlebell.

730
00:34:10,613 --> 00:34:13,225
I keep it in my trunk
for visits to clients' houses.

731
00:34:19,666 --> 00:34:20,623
[hissing]

732
00:34:26,107 --> 00:34:28,283
'Can you explain why
it has blood on it?'

733
00:34:31,243 --> 00:34:34,811
Ms. Chapin, I just spoke
to a man named Oleg Mostovoi.

734
00:34:34,898 --> 00:34:36,422
He's a loan shark.

735
00:34:36,509 --> 00:34:38,467
He said a few days
before the murder

736
00:34:38,554 --> 00:34:40,643
he loaned Kuzmenko $75,000

737
00:34:40,730 --> 00:34:44,995
and that Kuzmenko said he needed
the money to start his own gym.

738
00:34:45,083 --> 00:34:48,042
Is that why you killed him,
he was gonna compete with you?

739
00:34:48,129 --> 00:34:49,435
I heard from a realtor

740
00:34:49,522 --> 00:34:51,393
that Markus was
looking at gym spaces.

741
00:34:53,003 --> 00:34:55,702
My gym isn't doing
so well anymore

742
00:34:55,789 --> 00:34:58,922
so if he would...would have
left and...taken his clients

743
00:34:59,009 --> 00:35:00,968
I wouldn't have
been able to stay open.

744
00:35:01,055 --> 00:35:02,622
You confronted him.

745
00:35:04,189 --> 00:35:06,930
I begged him to stay.

746
00:35:07,017 --> 00:35:08,584
I mean, it's embarrassing.

747
00:35:08,671 --> 00:35:11,805
I...I said that
he shouldn't leave

748
00:35:11,892 --> 00:35:14,068
because of what
we meant to each other.

749
00:35:17,158 --> 00:35:19,595
And he just looked at me

750
00:35:19,682 --> 00:35:22,729
like he had no idea
what I was talking about.

751
00:35:22,816 --> 00:35:24,687
I mean,
he'd just forgotten.

752
00:35:26,950 --> 00:35:29,910
I suppose there had been
that many girls since.

753
00:35:32,739 --> 00:35:34,349
Oh, and then
he remembered later

754
00:35:34,436 --> 00:35:36,569
and he was charming,
of course, but..

755
00:35:40,312 --> 00:35:43,358
...I just...I just couldn't
help remember that look..

756
00:35:45,534 --> 00:35:48,015
...like I'd been deleted.

757
00:35:48,102 --> 00:35:51,061
And so I went to his house
and I waited for him.

758
00:35:58,678 --> 00:36:00,070
[grunts]

759
00:36:00,158 --> 00:36:03,073
[panting]

760
00:36:07,252 --> 00:36:11,473
You know, I-I don't
know why I did it. I..

761
00:36:11,560 --> 00:36:14,346
I've been on this
super strict diet.

762
00:36:14,433 --> 00:36:16,304
'You know, it messes
with your blood sugar.'

763
00:36:18,828 --> 00:36:20,874
Maybe that's it.

764
00:36:29,796 --> 00:36:32,364
Ray, you told me

765
00:36:32,451 --> 00:36:34,540
that this book
contained the names

766
00:36:34,627 --> 00:36:37,064
of many politically
prominent women

767
00:36:37,151 --> 00:36:40,415
who were carrying on affairs
with the victim in your case

768
00:36:40,502 --> 00:36:42,678
and your killer was
likely one of these women.

769
00:36:42,765 --> 00:36:46,769
Yeah, which is
a PR nightmare. Right?

770
00:36:46,856 --> 00:36:50,469
So I called the leadership
of both parties to warn them

771
00:36:50,556 --> 00:36:53,559
that we-we might have to act
on this because the one thing

772
00:36:53,646 --> 00:36:56,953
they hate more than bad news
is being surprised by it.

773
00:36:57,040 --> 00:37:00,218
But then...lo and behold

774
00:37:00,305 --> 00:37:05,919
I find out that-that-that
the names in here are forged.

775
00:37:06,006 --> 00:37:08,095
'There's no scandal.'

776
00:37:08,182 --> 00:37:09,705
'No, the book didn't even'

777
00:37:09,792 --> 00:37:11,794
belong to the victim.

778
00:37:11,881 --> 00:37:13,318
It's yours, I assume.

779
00:37:13,405 --> 00:37:16,625
Uh, yeah, if I could.
Uh, thank you.

780
00:37:16,712 --> 00:37:19,672
'I hate raising false alarms.'

781
00:37:19,759 --> 00:37:22,327
It makes me feel so foolish

782
00:37:22,414 --> 00:37:24,285
which I suppose was the point.

783
00:37:24,372 --> 00:37:26,069
- Well, maybe--
- Okay, if I may, sir--

784
00:37:26,156 --> 00:37:29,203
No, you may not.

785
00:37:29,290 --> 00:37:34,121
Now, Ray, I-I thought
that you could handle Jane.

786
00:37:34,208 --> 00:37:36,428
'Well, we all make mistakes.'

787
00:37:36,515 --> 00:37:39,866
But we'll find something else
for you to do. Don't worry.

788
00:37:53,096 --> 00:37:54,968
[sighs]

789
00:37:55,055 --> 00:37:57,927
Now...I have a problem.

790
00:37:59,842 --> 00:38:04,369
Because, uh, our urgent
need for your skills

791
00:38:04,456 --> 00:38:08,460
is precisely balanced
by my desire to fire you

792
00:38:08,547 --> 00:38:10,766
before you
destroy the bureau

793
00:38:10,853 --> 00:38:13,378
or my career.

794
00:38:13,465 --> 00:38:15,162
Or both.

795
00:38:17,077 --> 00:38:19,471
I understand.

796
00:38:19,558 --> 00:38:21,516
Mm. You're in a tough spot.

797
00:38:23,866 --> 00:38:26,086
You know who's always,
uh, been a very, uh

798
00:38:26,173 --> 00:38:29,002
healthy moderating
influence on me?

799
00:38:29,089 --> 00:38:30,830
[chuckles]

800
00:38:30,917 --> 00:38:33,354
Yeah. Of course.

801
00:38:33,441 --> 00:38:35,182
Lisbon.

802
00:38:35,269 --> 00:38:37,097
I'm sorry.

803
00:38:37,184 --> 00:38:38,925
Not possible.

804
00:38:39,012 --> 00:38:41,406
Well, then..

805
00:38:41,493 --> 00:38:43,799
...you will continue
to have a problem.

806
00:38:43,886 --> 00:38:46,759
[instrumental music]

807
00:38:54,593 --> 00:38:56,595
- Hey.
- Hey yourself.

808
00:38:56,682 --> 00:38:58,901
- You settling back in?
- Almost.

809
00:38:58,988 --> 00:39:01,513
- Yeah.
- How'd you do it?

810
00:39:01,600 --> 00:39:02,992
Get Bertram to come around?

811
00:39:03,079 --> 00:39:05,212
Oh, wasn't so hard.

812
00:39:05,299 --> 00:39:08,433
Rational discussion
between two reasonable men.

813
00:39:08,520 --> 00:39:11,566
[chuckling]
Rational discussion. I bet.

814
00:39:11,653 --> 00:39:13,046
Mm-hmm.

815
00:39:13,133 --> 00:39:16,615
But, uh...
by all means...go ahead.

816
00:39:16,702 --> 00:39:18,530
I'm sorry?

817
00:39:18,617 --> 00:39:20,270
Say it.
I won't get embarrassed.

818
00:39:20,358 --> 00:39:21,489
Say what?

819
00:39:22,447 --> 00:39:23,404
[whistling]

820
00:39:23,491 --> 00:39:25,232
Oh. You mean say thank you?

821
00:39:25,319 --> 00:39:29,845
Oh...you're welcome.
Was it so hard?

822
00:39:29,932 --> 00:39:32,195
The thing is,
not dealing with you every day

823
00:39:32,282 --> 00:39:33,980
has been very
restful for me.

824
00:39:34,067 --> 00:39:36,330
My back pain's gone.
I sleep really well.

825
00:39:36,417 --> 00:39:40,116
So, I'm actually not so sure
I'm grateful to be back.

826
00:39:40,203 --> 00:39:41,988
You're a terrible liar.

827
00:39:42,075 --> 00:39:44,991
No offense. Good, honest
people always bad liars.

828
00:39:45,078 --> 00:39:47,080
Which makes you what?

829
00:39:47,167 --> 00:39:49,082
Jane? Security just called.

830
00:39:49,169 --> 00:39:50,910
They've got someone
waiting for you downstairs.

831
00:39:50,997 --> 00:39:52,259
Name's Rosalind Harker.

832
00:39:52,346 --> 00:39:53,347
- Thanks, Grace.
- No problem.

833
00:39:53,434 --> 00:39:54,740
The woman that lived with

834
00:39:54,827 --> 00:39:56,568
Red John three
or four years ago?

835
00:39:56,655 --> 00:39:57,960
They didn't actually
live together.

836
00:39:58,047 --> 00:39:59,788
Uh, he was more
the gentleman caller.

837
00:39:59,875 --> 00:40:02,878
- What are you seeing her for?
- Great to have you back.

838
00:40:02,965 --> 00:40:04,880
What are you doing, Jane?

839
00:40:09,058 --> 00:40:10,190
[door squeaks]

840
00:40:10,277 --> 00:40:12,192
Thanks for agreeing
to this, Rosalind.

841
00:40:12,279 --> 00:40:15,151
I wasn't busy.
Besides, it sounded urgent.

842
00:40:15,238 --> 00:40:17,415
It is, in a way.

843
00:40:17,502 --> 00:40:19,504
Are you still playing piano?

844
00:40:19,591 --> 00:40:21,810
Not really. I'm tired
of playing for myself.

845
00:40:21,897 --> 00:40:24,378
- I think I need an audience.
- Okay, it's just here.

846
00:40:24,465 --> 00:40:26,772
And through a doorway
to the right.

847
00:40:29,514 --> 00:40:30,863
Something the matter?

848
00:40:30,950 --> 00:40:33,126
[inhales deeply]
Just a chill.

849
00:40:34,649 --> 00:40:36,869
- Let's go.
- Okay.

850
00:40:47,357 --> 00:40:50,186
[rattling]

851
00:40:55,888 --> 00:40:57,890
No one claimed the body?

852
00:40:57,977 --> 00:41:00,153
That's so sad.

853
00:41:00,240 --> 00:41:02,982
You'd think they'd have
done something with it.

854
00:41:03,069 --> 00:41:05,637
There must be a waiting
period or something.

855
00:41:05,724 --> 00:41:07,377
Yeah, something like that.

856
00:41:09,075 --> 00:41:11,599
You understand..

857
00:41:11,686 --> 00:41:15,821
he never called himself
Red John to me.

858
00:41:15,908 --> 00:41:20,652
The man I knew
was Roy Tagliaferro.

859
00:41:20,739 --> 00:41:23,568
Roy and Red John
are the same person.

860
00:41:23,655 --> 00:41:25,134
I'm sure of it.

861
00:41:31,314 --> 00:41:32,751
Is this him?

862
00:41:58,646 --> 00:42:00,300
I never met this man.

863
00:42:00,387 --> 00:42:02,345
I have no idea who he is.

864
00:42:04,783 --> 00:42:06,262
Thank you.

865
00:42:06,349 --> 00:42:09,265
[intense music]

866
00:42:11,572 --> 00:42:13,531
[rattling]

867
00:42:25,891 --> 00:42:28,110
[theme music]

